> Subject: Re: Prospective Research Assistant
> 
> Dear Apoorva,
> 
> Thank you for reaching out and for your thoughtful note. Your interests in coastal livelihoods, market access, climate adaptation, and economic resilience sound like a strong thematic fit with several areas of work at the University of Delaware.
> 
> I should clarify a few things about the relevant programs. The former Master’s in Marine Policy has transitioned into the new **M.S. in Environmental Science & Management**, which includes a **Marine track**:  
> https://www.udel.edu/academics/colleges/ceoe/prospective-students/graduate/environmental-science-and-management/
> 
> For students interested in the economics of climate impacts and adaptation, the most relevant programs are often the **M.S. in Environmental Economics** or the **Ph.D. in Environmental Economics**. The **Ph.D. in Marine Policy** can also be a good fit for non-traditional students or students with lighter quantitative backgrounds, depending on their goals.
> 
> At the moment, however, I am **not taking on new Ph.D. students or Graduate Research Assistants** for the upcoming admissions cycle. Also, the **M.S. in Environmental Economics** and **M.S. in Environmental Science & Management** are generally self-funded programs, rather than GRA-funded research placements.
> 
> Given your background and interests, I would encourage you to look closely at the Environmental Economics and ESM programs and consider applying if they align with your goals. If you do apply and are admitted, there may be opportunities to discuss research directions or potential advising fit at that stage.
> 
> Thank you again for your interest in my work and in UD, and I wish you the best with your graduate applications.
> 
> Best regards,  
> James Rising
